WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.763
Infinite-width theory, built on tools like the
Neural Tangent Kernel,

00:00:04.843 --> 00:00:09.483
has transformed how we understand deep network
initialisation and training,

00:00:09.563 --> 00:00:12.630
but it has only ever described a network's output.

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:17.036
This paper extends that theory to the network's
input-output Jacobian.

00:00:17.116 --> 00:00:24.400
The authors show that a multilayer perceptron and
its Jacobian jointly converge to a Gaussian process as width grows,

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:28.303
define a Jacobian Neural Tangent Kernel that
governs training,

00:00:28.383 --> 00:00:33.275
and prove that training with a Jacobian
regulariser, so-called robust training,

00:00:33.355 --> 00:00:37.555
behaves in the infinite-width limit like a simple
kernel regression.

00:00:37.635 --> 00:00:41.520
Along the way they explain, for the first time from
this angle,

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:45.800
why Jacobian regularisation makes networks both
accurate and robust.

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:47.652
Over the last few years,

00:00:47.732 --> 00:00:52.823
infinite-width theory has given us remarkably clean
answers about deep networks.

00:00:52.903 --> 00:00:57.413
Tools like the Neural Tangent Kernel tell us how a
network initialises,

00:00:57.493 --> 00:01:00.645
how it trains, and where gradient descent ends up.

00:01:00.725 --> 00:01:04.718
But there is a catch: all of this describes a
network's output.

00:01:04.798 --> 00:01:08.468
It says nothing about the network's input-output Jacobian,

00:01:08.548 --> 00:01:11.765
the object that captures how smooth the network is.

00:01:11.845 --> 00:01:18.488
And smoothness is exactly what we care about when
we want robustness to noise or to adversarial attacks.

00:01:18.568 --> 00:01:24.952
So a natural question is left wide open: can we
extend infinite-width theory to the Jacobian itself,

00:01:25.032 --> 00:01:28.120
and to training that deliberately regularises it?

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:32.668
Jacobian regularisation is one of those methods
that just works.

00:01:32.748 --> 00:01:37.579
Add a penalty on the network's input-output
Jacobian and you get a simple,

00:01:37.659 --> 00:01:40.499
strong defence against adversarial examples.

00:01:40.579 --> 00:01:43.950
The problem is that nobody could really explain why.

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:49.060
The theory that had cracked open ordinary training,
the infinite-width limit,

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:54.502
had never been pushed to cover the Jacobian, let
alone training that penalises it.

00:01:54.582 --> 00:02:00.940
This paper asks whether that same lens can finally
explain what Jacobian regularisation is doing.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:04.612
The paper makes four moves.

00:02:04.692 --> 00:02:07.984
First,

00:02:08.064 --> 00:02:11.356
it proves that at initialisation a multilayer
perceptron and its Jacobian jointly converge

00:02:11.436 --> 00:02:14.729
to a zero-mean Gaussian process,

00:02:14.809 --> 00:02:18.101
and it writes down that limiting kernel, the
Jacobian NNGP kernel,

00:02:18.181 --> 00:02:21.473
inductively over depth.

00:02:21.553 --> 00:02:24.845
Second, it defines a Jacobian Neural Tangent
Kernel, the JNTK,

00:02:24.925 --> 00:02:28.217
and proves it becomes deterministic at
initialisation and then stays constant throughout

00:02:28.297 --> 00:02:31.589
robust training as width grows.

00:02:31.669 --> 00:02:34.961
Third, because the kernel is constant,

00:02:35.041 --> 00:02:38.334
the training dynamics collapse to a linear
first-order differential equation whose infinite-time

00:02:38.414 --> 00:02:41.706
solution is a plain kernel regressor.

00:02:41.786 --> 00:02:45.078
And fourth,

00:02:45.158 --> 00:02:48.450
it validates all of this empirically and uses the
solution to study when accuracy and robustness go together.

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:50.087
Here is how it works.

00:02:50.167 --> 00:02:55.501
Robust training minimises the usual mean-squared
error plus lambda times a penalty on

00:02:55.581 --> 00:02:58.430
the network's Jacobian at each training point.

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:02.633
To handle the joint object, the network and its
Jacobian together,

00:03:02.713 --> 00:03:08.556
the authors recast the convergence question as a
tensor program and apply the Master theorem.

00:03:08.636 --> 00:03:15.497
They then define the finite JNTK from the
parameter-gradients of both the output and each Jacobian component.

00:03:15.577 --> 00:03:19.955
The crucial step is showing this kernel does not
move during training;

00:03:20.035 --> 00:03:24.349
that turns the learning dynamics into a linear
differential equation,

00:03:24.429 --> 00:03:29.317
and its infinite-time solution is a kernel
regression using the limiting JNTK,

00:03:29.397 --> 00:03:34.730
rescaled by a simple diagonal matrix that carries
the regularisation strength lambda.

00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:38.923
The experiments are deliberately small and controlled.

00:03:39.003 --> 00:03:42.815
To test the kernel-convergence claims,

00:03:42.895 --> 00:03:46.708
the authors use a synthetic 256-point dataset in
four dimensions,

00:03:46.788 --> 00:03:50.601
built to approximate an even covering of the sphere,

00:03:50.681 --> 00:03:54.494
and sweep every combination of width from 64 up to
8192 and depth one, two,

00:03:54.574 --> 00:03:58.386
and three.

00:03:58.466 --> 00:04:02.279
To test that the kernel really stays constant
during robust training,

00:04:02.359 --> 00:04:06.172
they switch to the Algerian forest fire dataset
from the UCI repository,

00:04:06.252 --> 00:04:10.065
224 points with eleven input features,

00:04:10.145 --> 00:04:13.957
treated as a plus-or-minus-one regression problem.

00:04:14.037 --> 00:04:17.850
The same setups let them line up robust training
against standard training head to head.

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:19.500
The theory holds up.

00:04:19.580 --> 00:04:21.254
As the network gets wider,

00:04:21.334 --> 00:04:26.585
the finite Jacobian NNGP kernel closes in on its
predicted deterministic limit,

00:04:26.665 --> 00:04:30.499
and the scaled finite JNTK converges to the limiting JNTK,

00:04:30.579 --> 00:04:33.265
matching the two initialisation theorems.

00:04:33.345 --> 00:04:35.087
And during robust training,

00:04:35.167 --> 00:04:42.712
the gap between the finite JNTK and its limit
shrinks monotonically with width at every training step we checked,

00:04:42.792 --> 00:04:46.289
which is exactly what the constancy theorem predicts.

00:04:46.369 --> 00:04:51.754
The largest networks tested don't fully nail the
bound because of compute limits,

00:04:51.834 --> 00:04:57.490
but the trend is unmistakable: wider is closer, in
precisely the way the theory says.

00:04:57.870 --> 00:05:02.998
The most striking finding comes from analysing the
kernel-regression solution.

00:05:03.078 --> 00:05:04.934
With Jacobian regularisation,

00:05:05.014 --> 00:05:09.073
the eigenfeatures that are more accurate are also
more robust;

00:05:09.153 --> 00:05:11.610
accuracy and robustness move together.

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:14.548
Standard training shows almost no such link,

00:05:14.628 --> 00:05:20.423
and it never produces the accurate-but-fragile
features seen in earlier finite-NTK work.

00:05:20.503 --> 00:05:25.163
So the regulariser isn't just trading a little
accuracy for robustness;

00:05:25.243 --> 00:05:26.365
it aligns the two.

00:05:26.445 --> 00:05:32.307
A separate check on the key full-rank assumption
shows it is fragile: the JNTK's smallest

00:05:32.387 --> 00:05:37.648
eigenvalue is far below the NTK's and only becomes
positive for deeper networks,

00:05:37.728 --> 00:05:41.520
depth eleven for GeLU but just six for the erf activation.

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:44.134
A few numbers anchor the study.

00:05:44.214 --> 00:05:48.539
Widths span 64 to 8192, powers of two from six to
thirteen.

00:05:48.619 --> 00:05:56.601
Every experiment is repeated ten times and reported
with ninety-five percent bootstrap confidence intervals,

00:05:56.681 --> 00:06:01.976
and the covariance estimates rest on a full million
Monte-Carlo samples.

00:06:02.056 --> 00:06:07.649
Robust training uses a Jacobian coefficient of
0.01, a scaling kappa of 0.1,

00:06:07.729 --> 00:06:09.665
and a learning rate of one.

00:06:09.745 --> 00:06:16.085
The full-rank condition first kicks in at depth
eleven for GeLU and depth six for erf,

00:06:16.165 --> 00:06:22.430
and the theory's width requirement scales like
N-squared times log-N to the twelve-L.

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:27.122
The bottom line is that infinite-width theory now
reaches the Jacobian.

00:06:27.202 --> 00:06:31.267
Train a wide network with a Jacobian regulariser
and, in the limit,

00:06:31.347 --> 00:06:35.721
it behaves like kernel regression with a Jacobian
Neural Tangent Kernel.

00:06:35.801 --> 00:06:42.774
And that same regulariser makes accuracy and
robustness pull in the same direction instead of fighting each other,

00:06:42.854 --> 00:06:47.599
giving us the first principled explanation, from
the infinite-width viewpoint,

00:06:47.679 --> 00:06:52.610
of why penalising the Jacobian yields networks that
are both accurate and robust.
